[^866]: If the soul follows the [{_appetitus naturalis_}]{lang=lat}, or goes not with another foot than that of nature.

[^867]: In the language of the Scholastics, Form was the passing from the potential to the actual. “Whatever is Act,” says Thomas Aquinas, {_Summa Theologica_}, Quaest. LXVI Art. 1, “whatever is Act is Form; [{_quod est actus est forma_}]{lang=lat}.” And again Form was divided into Substantial Form, which caused a thing to be; and Accidental Form, which caused it to be in a certain way, “as heat makes its subject not simply to be, but to be hot.”

  “The soul,” says the same Angelic Doctor, Quaest. LXXVI Art. 4, “is the substantial form of man; [{_anima est forma substantialis hominis_}]{lang=lat}.” It is segregate or distinct from matter, though united with it.

[^868]: “This” refers to the power that counsels, or the faculty of Reason.

[^869]: Accepts, or rejects like chaff.

